## Quality Guidelines

### 1. Documentation Quality Checklist

**Clarity:**
- [ ] Use clear, concise language
- [ ] Avoid jargon (or explain when necessary)
- [ ] Include examples
- [ ] Use diagrams where helpful

**Completeness:**
- [ ] All requirements documented
- [ ] All edge cases considered
- [ ] Security implications addressed
- [ ] Performance implications addressed
- [ ] Testing strategy defined

**Traceability:**
- [ ] Links to requirements
- [ ] Links to related documents
- [ ] References to code
- [ ] Decision rationale documented

**Maintainability:**
- [ ] Kept up-to-date with code
- [ ] Version controlled
- [ ] Review process defined
- [ ] Owner identified

### 2. Diagram Standards

**Types of Diagrams:**
- **Architecture Diagrams**: System components and relationships
- **Sequence Diagrams**: Interaction flows over time
- **Entity Relationship Diagrams**: Database schema
- **Flow Charts**: Decision flows and processes
- **State Diagrams**: State transitions

**Diagram Tools:**
- PlantUML / Mermaid (text-based, version controlled)
- Draw.io / Lucidchart (visual editors)
- C4 Model (for architecture diagrams)

**Diagram Guidelines:**
- Keep diagrams simple and focused
- Include legend if needed
- Use consistent notation
- Date diagrams
- Store with documentation

## Review and Approval Process

### 1. Design Review Checklist

**Technical Review:**
- [ ] Architecture sound
- [ ] Scalable solution
- [ ] Secure design
- [ ] Performance acceptable
- [ ] Maintainable code structure

**Business Review:**
- [ ] Meets requirements
- [ ] Delivers business value
- [ ] Acceptable timeline
- [ ] Acceptable cost
- [ ] Risk mitigation adequate

**Team Review:**
- [ ] Understandable by team
- [ ] Testable design
- [ ] Deployable solution
- [ ] Monitorable implementation

### 2. Approval Process

1. **Draft**: Author creates initial design
2. **Team Review**: Team provides feedback
3. **Revision**: Author incorporates feedback
4. **Architecture Review**: Tech lead/architect reviews
5. **Stakeholder Review**: Business stakeholders review
6. **Approval**: Formal approval to proceed
7. **Implementation**: Build according to design
8. **Retrospective**: Review design after implementation

## Documentation Maintenance

### 1. When to Update Documentation

**Triggers for Update:**
- Requirements change
- Architecture evolves
- Major refactoring
- New patterns emerge
- Lessons learned from implementation
- Security vulnerabilities discovered

### 2. Documentation Versioning

- Version control all documentation
- Tag documentation with code releases
- Archive old versions
- Link documentation to code commits
- Update changelog

### 3. Documentation Ownership

- Assign owner to each document
- Regular review schedule
- Update documentation in code reviews
- Include documentation in definition of done

---

**Remember**: Good documentation is:
- **Accurate**: Reflects current reality
- **Useful**: Helps developers make decisions
- **Maintained**: Kept up-to-date
- **Accessible**: Easy to find and understand
- **Concise**: No unnecessary detail

Document decisions and trade-offs, not just facts. Future developers (including your future self) will thank you.
